   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to updating certain\nreferences with respect to the Troy Industrial Development Authority,\neliminating an automatic corporate termination clause when all bonds have been\nrepaid, allowing the Troy Industrial Development Authority to deposit and\nmanage its own funds and accounts, adding certain retail restrictions and\nmaking certain technical corrections",
    "basePrintNo": "S7082",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7082-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1951, 1952, 1953, 1955, 1956, 1957, 1958 & 1967, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7082\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to updating\ncertain references with respect to the Troy Industrial Development\nAuthority, eliminating an automatic corporate termination clause when\nall bonds have been repaid, allowing the Troy Industrial Development\nAuthority to deposit and manage its own funds and accounts, adding\ncertain retail restrictions and making certain technical corrections\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill makes corrections to the enabling act of the Troy Industrial\nDevelopment Authority (TIDA).\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 1 of section 1951 of the public authori-\nties law to make a technical correction.\n\nSection two amends section 1952 of the public authorities law to make\ntechnical corrections and to apply section nineteen hundred sixty-seven.\n\nSection three amends section 1953 of the public authorities law to make\ntechnical corrections and to provide definitions and provisions relating\nto \"retail sales.\" \"Tourism destination\" is also defined.\n\nSection four amends subdivisions 4 and 7 of the section 1953 of the\npublic authorities law to make technical corrections.\n\nSection five amends subdivision 1 of section 1955 of the public authori-\nties law to make technical corrections.\n\nSection six amends section 1956 of the public authorities law to make\ntechnical corrections.\n\nSection seven amends section 1957 of the public authorities law to make\ntechnical corrections.\n\nSection eight amends paragraph (i) of subdivision 3 of section 1.958 of\nthe public authorities law to make a technical correction.\n\nSection nine amends section 1967 of the public authorities law to\ninclude the termination of straight-lease transactions to an event that\ntriggers the termination of the authority.\n\nSection ten establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe bill updates the enabling act of the Troy Industrial Development\nAuthority (TIDA), including correcting some typographical errors and\ncorrections to references associated with the City of Troy's former\nstructure of government, which included a City Manager. The bill\nincludes provisions that were adopted for all New York Industrial Devel-\nopment Agencies governed under Article 18-A of the General; Municipal\nLaw in 2012 under Chapter 373 of the Laws of 2012. Pursuant to that act,\nwhich was effective October 15, 2012 and deemed effective January 1,\n2002 as a savings clause, the provisions of GML\n\nSection 882 were amended to allow IDAs to remain active as long as they\nhave at least one active straight-lease transaction. That chapter also\ndissolved many inactive or defunct IDAs, Urban Renewal Agencies and\nother authorities.\n\nPrior to this act, IDAs governed by this GML Section 882 were at risk of\ntermination if all of their bonds or notes were redeemed or cancelled.\nWith many active IDAs having their bonds refinanced or paid off, the\nprovisions of Chapter 373 of the Laws of 2012 preserved IDAs governed by\nGML Article 18-A from unintended dissolution and remain active and\nproductive economic development tools for New York's municipalities. As\na component of budget legislation adopted in early 2013, Chapter 59 of\n2013 (part J effective 3/28/13) restored certain retail restrictions on\nthe provision of IDA financial assistance that had went into \"sunset.\"\nin 2008 along with certain other GML Article 18-A provisions. The prior\nretail restrictions were added back into the GML with the intended\npurpose of restoring the same for all New York IDAs. However, the\nprovisions of the two chapters described above did not include 2 IDAs\nestablished under the Public Authorities Law (PAL), including TIDA and\nthe Auburn Industrial Development Authority (AIDA).\n\nLater in 2013, a bill was introduced and adopted to amend the AIDA\nenabling act (Chapter 304 of the Laws of 2013), which preserved that\nIDA's existence as long as it has any active straight-lease trans-\nactions, and also restored the prior retail restrictions on the\nprovision of financial assistance by AIDA. Until now, no similar or\ncompanion bill has been enacted to provide TIDA with the amended termi-\nnation language or re-instate the retail restrictions. This bill would\naccomplish these intended purposes and also address certain technical\ncorrections to TIDA's enabling act codified under the PAL.  As noted\nwithin the Introducer's memorandum of support for the AIDA amendments\nunder Chapter 304 of the Laws of 2013, that law was adopted as \"a chap-\nter amendment to Budget Bill 5.2609-D Part J of the 2013-14 budget_ The\ncity of Auburn was inadvertently excluded from changes in the law\npertaining to IDA tax credits which this bill will rectify.\"\n\nThis bill corrects the same inadvertent exclusion, albeit somewhat\nbelatedly, and will place TIDA on equal footing with all New York IDAs,\nincluding AIDA.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
